  "schema_version": "bookstory.v1",
  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Dies irae shows early but meaningful reach for a scholarly book in music and the humanities: it has been downloaded 1,614 times through OAPEN, is available as open access, and has already generated a small amount of public attention in Crossref\u2019s records. The citation record is still at zero, so the strongest evidence here is use and visibility rather than scholarly uptake.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Pieter Berg\u00e9\u2019s Dies irae, published by Leuven University Press in 2021 and co-authored with Jan Christiaens, is positioned at the intersection of art, humanities, theology, and philosophy. The book introduces readers to the requiem tradition and its long history in Western music, with an accessible approach that does not require musical training. Its open-access availability appears to be supporting reach: OAPEN metrics record 1,614 downloads, indicating sustained reader interest in the work.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Beyond usage, the book has a small but visible footprint in public-facing metadata. Crossref records two mentions, which suggests some level of public discourse or indexing visibility, though the available record does not support a larger media story. The book is also listed as open access in repository records, strengthening its accessibility and likely contributing to the download pattern observed.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">At the same time, the scholarly citation record is still at zero in OpenAlex. That means the current evidence points more to readership and discoverability than to documented citation impact. For a book published in 2021, this is a relatively early-stage profile, and the record should be read as developing rather than settled.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Teaching-adoption evidence in the digest is limited to library holdings data from WorldCat, which indicates bibliographic presence rather than confirmed course use. No event data are available, and the data-citation checks were successful, but the digest does not surface a broader citation-to-dataset story. Overall, Dies irae already shows clear signs of being read and noticed, with open access likely playing an important role in that visibility.</p>",
  "narrative_source": "template"
